## Four assertions that carry more weight than the rest

1. **`syncToken` is asserted ABSENT.** `expect(searchParams.has("syncToken")).toBe(false)`. Google returns 400 when a sync token accompanies a bounded window, so this is the one parameter whose absence is the contract. Proving a negative needs the outbound request object, which is exactly what the injected `fetch` provides and what `fetchMock` — removed from the pool in 0.13.x — no longer could.
2. **The mirror inventory is tested by SERIALIZING the DTO and searching it**, not field by field. `expect(JSON.stringify(dto)).not.toContain("someone@example.com")` catches a leak into any field, including one added later; a field-by-field assertion would pass right over a new one.
3. **A partial failure returns the healthy events AND names the failed calendar.** Both halves are asserted in the same test, because either alone is the bug: dropping the events loses real commitments, and hiding the failure produces a day that looks complete and is not.
4. **`title` is asserted NOT to be a pt-BR fallback.** `expect(dto.title).not.toMatch(/sem t[ií]tulo/i)` — the mapper knows an event has no title but not what an untitled event should *read as*, and ADR-0009 keeps `src/shared` free of visible copy. The test stops a well-meaning future edit from moving a design decision into a data layer.

