## RED verification (test-first)

```
npx vitest run test/google-events.test.ts test/google-calendar-client.test.ts test/google-calendar-routes.test.ts
```

Exit code **1**, 3 suites failed, 38 tests failed. The three failure signatures each name a seam-set artifact:

- `Cannot find module '../src/shared/google-events'` — Task 2's mapper.
- `TypeError: refreshAccessToken is not a function` (also `listCalendars`, `listEvents`) — Task 3's client operations. The module exists from phase 2, so the import resolves and the missing exports surface as undefined rather than a module error.
- `D1_ERROR: incomplete input` — Task 4's `google_calendar_selections` table, not yet exported from the schema, so Drizzle builds a `delete from ` with no table name. The same murky signature phase 2 recorded; written down again rather than dressed up.

Every symbol referenced is named in the APPROVED plan's task prose: `toCalendarEventDto`, `eventWindow` (Task 2); `refreshAccessToken`, `listCalendars`, `listEvents` (Task 3); `googleCalendarSelections` (Task 4); the three route paths (Tasks 5 and 6).

## Fixtures come from the owner's real calendar

The mapper's fixtures are not invented shapes. They are what chore C12 pulled out of the owner's account on 2026-08-25: an all-day event (`momo`, `start.date`), a timed one with an offset (`Daily - Super Ensino`, `start.dateTime`), and one with no `summary` at all. Testing against shapes that actually occurred is the difference between covering the API surface and covering this owner's calendar.

## Scope limits

- No production code written.
- No UI, no rendering, no pt-BR copy — phase 4. The suite asserts the mapper does NOT invent Portuguese copy, which is the inverse obligation.
- No test of the FR-042 export exclusion; the export does not exist until unit 5.
- Nothing is asserted against the real Google. These tests fake every outbound call; the real round-trip is a separate, human step.

## Review round 1 — CHANGES_REQUESTED, and the bug my own fix contained

Three findings, all upheld:

1. **`R-TRIVIAL-ASSERT`** — "answers distinguishably when there is no connection" asserted only
   `not.toBe(200)`, which a 500 crash satisfies identically to a deliberate fail-closed answer. Worse,
   nothing anywhere asserted the not-connected response actually DIFFERS from the Google-down one,
   which is the plan's literal Task 5 obligation. Now two tests: one pinning 4xx, one comparing both
   responses' `reason` discriminator.
2. **`R-AC-COVERAGE` on AC-13** — per-call `GET` assertions cannot carry a universal negative. Covered
   now by `test/source-invariants.test.ts`, which reads the source tree in the `docs` project (plain
   Node, because workerd has no `node:fs`).
3. **`R-AC-COVERAGE` on AC-A9** — the manifest claimed coverage that did not exist. Closed by
   `test/day-item-event.test.ts`.

**The structural guard shipped broken, and only a deliberate violation exposed it.** After writing
`source-invariants`, a violating `events` POST was planted in `client.ts` — and the guard **passed**.
Cause: the comment-stripping regex `/\/\/.*$/gm` eats everything after the `//` in `https://`, so the
check saw a file containing no Google endpoints at all. `scripts/check-mirror-inventory.mjs` — written
one round earlier to replace a *different* defeatable check — carried the identical bug. Both now
strip `//` only when it is not preceded by `:`, and the guard was re-verified in both directions:
**fails** with the planted violation, **passes** without it.

The lesson is not the regex. It is that a green guard proves nothing until it has been made to fail
on purpose.

## Correction round (2026-08-31 — AC-A8 test consolidation)

**Trigger:** two independent retrospective `post-green-reviewer` findings named the same test without
prescribing the same fix. `PRPs/reports/google-calendar-read/phase-3/test-review.json` flagged it
directly as a "RESIDUAL GAP (inherited, not introduced)": `test/google-calendar-routes.test.ts`
`"reports a dead credential distinguishably from a transient outage"` still used
`expect(res.status).not.toBe(200)` — "the same weak pattern that WAS fixed for the analogous
`/calendars` test in the same file". `PRPs/reports/google-calendar-read/phase-4/test-review.json`'s
`coverage_finding` reached the same test from a different angle while checking phase 4's delivery
claim that partial-failure coverage "rests on ... two route tests": it found "Only ONE of the two
demonstrates the per-calendar mechanism" — the other "stubs the token refresh itself, which fails
EVERY calendar identically before any per-calendar request — the sibling total-failure state, not the
partial one." The task was filed as "strengthen the weak assertion"; that remedy was checked against
source before being applied, and found to be the wrong one.

**Investigation, verified directly against source before acting (not taken on either report's word):**

1. **Strictly weaker duplicate.** The flagged test's stub — `url.pathname === "/token"` → `invalid_grant`
   / 400, else 500 — is the same logic as `"answers 409 on invalid_grant, so the screen can say
   RECONNECT"` (`describe("a dead credential is not a transient outage (AC-A8)")`, same file, line
   270). Same `connect()` precondition, same route (`GET /events`), same stub predicate. The survivor
   asserts `toBe(409)` and `reason === "invalid_grant"`; the flagged test asserted only `not.toBe(200)`
   plus a regex match against the response text. Anything that satisfies the survivor's assertions (a
   409 JSON body naming `invalid_grant`) also satisfies the flagged test's — including the exact
   `{status: 502}` regression `accessTokenFor`'s own source comment
   (`src/worker/routes/google.ts:166-180`) says was fixed, because `refreshed.reason` stays
   `"invalid_grant"` regardless of which status the route attaches to it. The flagged test could not
   have caught a reintroduction of that regression, so strengthening it in place would have produced a
   near-exact copy of an already-existing, already-stronger test (its own pathology, `R-DUPLICATE`).
2. **Wrong describe block.** The flagged test lived inside `"GET /api/google/events — a partial
   failure is never a shorter list (AC-A8)"`, but its stub fails token refresh, and
   `googleRoutes.get("/events")` (`src/worker/routes/google.ts:263-265`) calls `accessTokenFor` and
   returns before the per-calendar loop (`:270-286`) ever starts. It is a total-failure case wearing a
   partial-failure block's heading — confirmed by reading the route handler directly, not inferred from
   either report.
3. **A real, previously-uncovered boundary exists in the same block.** The per-calendar loop had no
   test for "every selected calendar's `listEvents` fails, but the credential itself is fine" — only
   the 1-of-2 case (`"returns the healthy calendar's events AND reports the failed one"`). Reading the
   loop directly (`src/worker/routes/google.ts:270-286`) confirms it returns `c.json({ events,
   failedCalendars, window })` — HTTP 200, Hono's default — with `events: []` and every id in
   `failedCalendars` when ALL selected calendars fail transiently. This is the sharpest form of the
   block's own claim: an all-failed day and a credential-level outage (409/502, asserted elsewhere)
   must stay distinguishable, and nothing proved the all-fail case actually renders as 200 rather than
   accidentally inheriting an error status.

**Decision: `REDUNDANT_TEST_REMOVED`, immediately filled by `NEW_TEST_REQUIRED`, both against Plan
AC-A8 / PRD AC-11, both at the same location** (`test/google-calendar-routes.test.ts`, inside the
`"a partial failure is never a shorter list (AC-A8)"` describe block). Not `EXISTING_TEST_UPDATED`:
AC-A8's contract did not change, and the old test's claimed behaviour was not "updated" so much as
retired in favour of coverage that already exists, stronger, elsewhere in this file. Not a bare
deletion either (option (a) was considered and rejected): the same investigation surfaced a real,
previously-uncovered behaviour in scope of the very same AC, so filling it beat leaving the block
thinner than it needs to be.

| Op | Classification | Test (file:function) | Justification |
|----|-----------------|-----------------------|----------------|
| DELETE | REDUNDANT_TEST_REMOVED | `test/google-calendar-routes.test.ts` — `it("reports a dead credential distinguishably from a transient outage")` (was line 434) | Proven duplicate, strictly weaker, of `test/google-calendar-routes.test.ts:270` `it("answers 409 on invalid_grant, so the screen can say RECONNECT")` — same route, same precondition, same stub predicate; survivor named. Also physically misplaced (Investigation #2): its total-failure stub never reached the per-calendar mechanism the enclosing block is about. |

The freed slot, same file, same describe block, now carries `it("answers 200 when every calendar
fails, not an outage-shaped status")` — `NEW_TEST_REQUIRED` against Plan AC-A8 / PRD AC-11 (outcome
row updated above): two selected calendars, token refresh succeeds, every `events.list` call answers
503, asserts `res.status === 200`, `body.events` is `[]`, and `body.failedCalendars` names both ids.
This is observable-outcome testing (HTTP status + response shape) naming no internal function, and it
is discriminative against every other test in the file: distinct from the 1-of-2 case (all fail here,
not one), distinct from the credential-dead cases (the token call succeeds here). Mocking stays
confined to the same injected-`fetch` boundary every other test in this file already uses; no chained
mocks, no was-called-only assertion.

**Anti-weakening check.** AC-A8/AC-11's "dead credential is distinguishable from a transient outage"
guarantee is NOT weakened by this removal: it remains fully covered, more strongly, by the untouched
survivor at line 270 and its two untouched siblings (lines 285, 297) in the same describe block, none
of which this correction touched. No in-scope AC lost coverage; AC-A8 gained a genuinely new,
previously-absent case.

**Test count, exactly.** One `it()` removed, one `it()` added, same file:
`test/google-calendar-routes.test.ts` carries the same number of test cases (25) it had immediately
before this correction. No other file was touched. The project-wide baseline (598 passing / 0 failing)
should therefore be unaffected — but this agent invocation carries no Bash tool and did not and could
not run `npm test` or `npm run check` to confirm it. **Whoever runs `/relay-test-write-review` next
must run both and record the actual counts** before flipping this manifest's status.

**Formatting outcome.** Not run by this agent invocation — no Bash tool available (the same constraint
the phase-5 manifest's own correction round recorded). The edited test was hand-formatted against
`.prettierrc` (`printWidth: 100`, `singleQuote: false`, `trailingComma: "all"`, `tabWidth: 2`) and the
surrounding file's existing conventions — e.g. mirroring the exact `.insert(...).values(...)` line
breaks the sibling test three cases above it already uses. **Whoever runs `/relay-test-write-review`
